// Source document: car_free_op.md
// matcha analysis: transcribed by AI (Claude Opus 5.5). Matcha-D is an unofficial extension.

:: First, safety
// Source: para 2 "Most serious pedestrian injuries in our city happen in the downtown core, and nearly all of them involve private cars."
// Supplied premise: the text moves from "involve private cars" to "removing the cars would remove most of the danger"; the step that the cars are the cause is left implicit.
#safety <= the downtown core: (
    %private cars are the source of most of the danger to pedestrians downtown <= (most serious pedestrian injuries in our city happen in the downtown core & nearly all serious pedestrian injuries downtown involve private cars)@injuries that involve private cars are caused by the private cars
// Source: para 2 "Removing the cars would remove most of the danger."
) >> removing the cars would remove most of the danger as safety

:: Second, business
// Source: para 3 "Shop owners fear that customers who drive will stop coming."
#shop owners' objection <= the car-free center: (
    %customers who drive will stop coming <= shop owners fear that customers who drive will stop coming
// Paraphrase: "would hurt local shops" is the objection implied by "rather than hurt them".
) >> a car-free center would hurt local shops as shopfear

// Source: para 3 "But surveys in other cities suggest that pedestrians visit shops more often and spend about as much per month as drivers do."
#business^surveys in other cities <= the car-free center: (
    %pedestrians are at least as valuable customers as drivers are <= surveys in other cities suggest that pedestrians visit shops more often and spend about as much per month as drivers do@the survey results from other cities hold for our city
// Source: para 3 "A car-free center could therefore help local shops rather than hurt them."
) >> a car-free center could help local shops rather than hurt them as business !> $shopfear

:: Third, air quality
// Source: para 4 "Traffic is the largest source of nitrogen dioxide downtown, and the levels on our main street regularly exceed the national guideline."
// Supplied premise: the paragraph states no conclusion and does not say that private cars make up the traffic; both are supplied so that the paragraph can serve as a reason.
#air quality <= the downtown core: (
    %a car-free center would cut the main source of nitrogen dioxide downtown <= traffic is the largest source of nitrogen dioxide downtown@private cars make up most of the downtown traffic
    %the nitrogen dioxide downtown is a problem worth acting on <= the nitrogen dioxide levels on our main street regularly exceed the national guideline
) >> a car-free center would improve air quality downtown as air

:: The plan
// Source: para 6 "For these reasons, the city council should adopt a car-free downtown plan." (para 1 states the same thesis with "within five years")
#car-free downtown <= the city: (
    $safety
    $business
    $air
) >> the city council should adopt a car-free downtown plan as plan

:: The objection from disability
// Source: para 5 "Some will object that people with disabilities depend on cars. This is a real concern"
#disability objection <= the plan: (
    +%people with disabilities depend on cars <= some will object that people with disabilities depend on cars
) >> not the city council should adopt a car-free downtown plan as disability !> $plan

// Source: para 5 "but it is an argument for exceptions, not against the plan: permits for disabled drivers, delivery vehicles and emergency services can be kept."
#exceptions <= the plan: (
    %the concern can be met by exceptions within the plan <= permits for disabled drivers, delivery vehicles and emergency services can be kept@permits for disabled drivers meet the needs of people with disabilities
) >> the concern of people with disabilities is an argument for exceptions, not against the plan as exceptions !> $disability
